window.PROJECTS_DATA=[
{id:'ai-butler',highlight:'Led the in-room smart speaker redesign; 7 hotels renewed or upgraded within a month of launch',coverNatural:true,projectType:'Hardware–Software Integration · Interaction Design',timeline:'2023 – Now',platform:'Android App',timelineFirst:true,labels:['Hardware–Software Integration','Interaction Design'],tag:'Smart Speaker',pairTag:'Voice Interaction Design',year:'Flagship Project',title:'AVA Smart Speaker Interaction System',coverHeadline:'Helping guests understand the AI quickly; 7 hotels renewed or upgraded within a month',coverSub:'I led the redesign of an in-room AI smart speaker, so guests know what the AI is doing without a tutorial, and gave the most visible spot on the home screen to what hotels want to promote.',
scope:'• Led the speaker’s interface redesign, finding the real problem through surveys, sales feedback and usability tests\n• Chose to hint the AI’s state with text and animation in the real flow, instead of a long tutorial\n• Redesigned the home screen from usage data across hotels, giving the most visible spot to hotel content\n• Unified each TV vendor’s control logic with engineers, the PM and the Japanese sales team\n• Produced every interaction, meditation and holiday animation from scratch in After Effects',collaborators:'PM · Frontend & backend engineers · LLM engineers · Japanese sales team',
oneLiner:'I led the redesign of an in-room AI smart speaker, so guests know what the AI is doing without a tutorial, and gave the most visible spot on the home screen to what hotels want to promote.',
blocks:[
{heading:'Overview',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'**The situation:** Guests felt the AI “wasn’t smart enough”, but the real issue was that they couldn’t tell what state it was in. Hotels also reported that the home screen’s biggest tile, music, brought them no revenue.\n\n**The key decision:** No tutorial: hint the AI’s state with text and animation inside the real flow. On the home screen, keep the grid and give the most visible spot to hotel content.\n\n**The result:** 7 hotels renewed or upgraded within a month of launch. Clicks on promoted content grew 233% at Mitsui Garden Hotel, and marketing clicks grew 2.9× at Hotel Cham Cham.'},{noImage:true,text:'The speaker sits on the desk in every room, and it’s the product hotels see first. If guests can’t figure out how to talk to it, they stop using it, and hotels have no reason to renew. If the home screen only drives music, it adds no value to the hotel’s business.'},{noImage:true,caption:'Before: the old interface. After: the refreshed interface, with clearer hierarchy and wording.',compare:[{label:'Dial pad',before:'slot-assets/ava-ba-dial-before.png',after:'slot-assets/ava-ba-dial-after.png'},{label:'In a call',before:'slot-assets/ava-ba-call-before.png',after:'slot-assets/ava-ba-call-after.webp'},{label:'Delivery service',before:'slot-assets/ava-ba-delivery-before.png',after:'slot-assets/ava-ba-delivery-after.png'},{label:'Clock',before:'slot-assets/ava-ba-clock-before.png',after:'slot-assets/ava-ba-clock-after.png'}]}]},
{heading:'Challenge',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,title:'Guests didn’t think the AI was dumb. They just couldn’t read its state.',text:'• **Every state played the same animation.** While the AI was thinking or answering, the mic was off, but guests couldn’t tell and kept repeating themselves.\n• **The biggest tile was music.** 35–46% of taps went to music across hotels, while the content hotels wanted to promote went unseen.\n• **The full-image button looked like a picture.** It was the second most-tapped item, yet the grid made it look decorative, with no call-to-action.'}]},
{heading:'Discovery',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'I combined surveys and sales feedback, usability tests, usage data from several hotels, issues hotels raised in regular reviews, and interviews with our Japanese sales team.'},{imgId:'ai-butler-call-states',natural:true,title:'Hint the AI’s state in the real flow, instead of a tutorial',text:'**Decision:** A first-use tutorial was too long and stopped guests from exploring. Instead, the screen stays still and shows “I’m listening” while the AI listens, animation appears only while it processes, and a home-screen push message offers an optional guide.\n**Trade-off:** Because the device has a screen, guests naturally treat it as a touch device, and some may never realize they can talk to it.'},{imgId:'ai-butler-home-custom',title:'Keep the home-screen grid, and change only the visual layer',text:'**Decision:** I let the background hold an image, made button transparency adjustable, and enlarged the image button, so the screen is led by the hotel’s brand imagery instead of scattered tiles.\n**Trade-off:** Keeping the existing grid meant the layout couldn’t be fully rethought, in exchange for no redevelopment.'},{imgId:'ai-butler-iot',title:'Channels first for TV control, with input sources as a second layer',text:'**Decision:** Japanese TVs add a layer of input sources above channels. I first planned a custom flow for Japan, but interviews with our Japanese sales team showed channels are how people remember what to watch, so channels became the main view and input sources moved to a second-level menu.\n**Trade-off:** I used icons instead of “ON / OFF” text so translations wouldn’t break the small layout. Icons are less explicit than words, so each one had to be clear on its own.'}]},
{heading:'Solution',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'**Guests know when to speak.**\nThe screen stays still with “I’m listening” while listening, and animates only while processing.\n\n**Learning is optional, not forced.**\nA home-screen push message offers a guide for those who want it, without blocking normal use.\n\n**The home screen sells for the hotel.**\nBrand imagery leads the screen, and the most visible spot goes to dining and promotions.\n\n**One TV interface works across vendors and countries.**\nChannels come first and input sources sit one layer down, so it fits Japanese and Singaporean hotels.\n\n**Meditation runs from one sentence.**\nScreen, sound and room lighting change together, and the screen steps back so guests can relax; it became a sales highlight.\n\n**The state system carried into the phone module.**\nWhen calling moved into the phone module, the same states carried over without redesign.'},{noImage:true,caption:'Home screen before and after the redesign.',compare:[{label:'Home screen',before:'slot-assets/ava-ba-home-before.webp',after:'slot-assets/ava-ba-home-after.webp'}]},{imgId:'ai-butler-wellness-video',ratio:'4/3'},{imgId:'ai-butler-phone-merge',natural:true},{imgId:'ai-butler-phone-merge-r',natural:true}]},
{heading:'Process',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'Surveys, sales feedback and usability tests → define the real problem → design states and home screen → align with engineers, the PM and the Japanese sales team → produce animations in After Effects → demo to hotels and launch\n\n• **What I decided:** No tutorial; hinting state in the real flow; keeping the grid while handing the top spot to hotel content; channels before input sources.\n• **What I owned:** Interface and state design, the home-screen system, and every interaction, meditation and holiday animation, built from scratch in After Effects. The new interface and flow were demoed directly to hotels during renewals.'},{pairIds:['ai-butler-festive-anim','ai-butler-festive-anim-2','ai-butler-festive-anim-3','ai-butler-festive-anim-4'],caption:'Other motion work: holiday animations for marketing.'}]},
{heading:'Impact',type:'cards',items:[{compact:true,stat:'7',statUnit:'hotels',title:'Hotels renewed or upgraded within a month',noImage:true},{compact:true,stat:'+233%',statUnit:'after the home redesign',title:'Clicks on promoted content, Mitsui Garden Hotel',noImage:true},{compact:true,stat:'2.9×',statUnit:'after the home redesign',title:'Marketing clicks, Hotel Cham Cham',noImage:true},{compact:true,stat:'+1',statUnit:'new client',title:'New hotel in Singapore, won through the meditation feature',noImage:true}]},
{type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,small:true,title:'Hotel Feedback',text:'“Now housekeeping requests get a fast response, restaurant reservations are easy to arrange, and guest preferences are recorded automatically — all thanks to the leading AI technology of the Aiello Voice Assistant.” — M Social Hotel Phuket, Thailand.'}]},
{type:'text',items:['**What I would do next:** Track how often guests repeat themselves or interrupt the device after launch, to measure how much the clearer states helped.\n\n**The biggest change:** Guests can read the AI from across the room, and the home screen became space that works for the hotel’s business.']}
]},
{id:'aca-ai',highlight:'Redesigned the AI voice agent’s ordering flow; orders completed without a transfer rose from 0% to 95%',coverRatio:'2/1',projectType:'UX Design · Data Analysis',timeline:'2026 – Now',platform:'Phone',timelineFirst:true,labels:['Voice Interaction Design','Conversation Flow Design'],tag:'AI Phone Agent',pairTag:'Conversation Flow Design',tag3:'Test Scenario Planning',year:'Flow Optimization',title:'AI Voice Agent for Hotel In-Room Dining',coverHeadline:'Letting the AI finish in-room dining orders, instead of transferring every one to staff',coverSub:'I redesigned the AI’s ordering conversations from real call data. Orders completed without a transfer rose from 0% to 95%, taking real work off hotel staff.',
scope:'• Led the ordering conversation design, defining the AI’s scope from real call data\n• Decided which cases the AI handles and which go to staff, and gave the PM the reasoning to convince hotels\n• Turned conversation issues into rules the AI follows and specs engineers build from\n• Built a validation method, using a Claude Skill to check what each AI reply was based on',collaborators:'PM · Data analyst · Backend & LLM engineers · Sales',
oneLiner:'I redesigned the AI’s ordering conversations from real call data. Orders completed without a transfer rose from 0% to 95%, taking real work off hotel staff.',
blocks:[
{heading:'Overview',type:'text',sideChart:[
{label:'Human agent request',pct:100,value:'27%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'In-room dining',pct:59,value:'16%',color:'#85B7EB',bold:true},
{label:'Laundry',pct:41,value:'11%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Other',pct:41,value:'11%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Amenities',pct:33,value:'9%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Room Reservation',pct:33,value:'9%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Technical Repair',pct:19,value:'5%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Other Topic',pct:15,value:'4%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Hotel Policy',pct:11,value:'3%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'F&B Facility',pct:7,value:'2%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Transport&Travel',pct:4,value:'1%',color:'#B0B0B4'}
],items:['**The situation:** In-room dining is the second most common reason guests call (16%), but the AI transferred every order to staff, so their workload didn’t drop at all.\n\n**The key decision:** Instead of making the AI handle everything, let it complete as much of the order as possible, and leave payment, which is complex and prone to complaints, to staff.\n\n**The result:** At Crowne Plaza Changi Airport in Singapore, orders completed without a transfer rose from 0% to 95% on average, about 600 orders a month.','Ordering ties directly to a hotel’s F&B revenue, and it’s the feature hotels care about most. If the AI can only answer calls but not take orders, hotels are paying for a call forwarder. Staff stay stretched, and it becomes hard to convince hotels to renew or expand.']},
{heading:'Challenge',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,title:'Guests weren’t unwilling to order through the AI. The AI gave up at the first surprise.',text:'• **It transferred at any off-menu question.** A guest asks for pasta without garlic; the AI can’t answer, transfers the call, and the order stops.\n• **It sounded like a system, not hotel staff.** It read out error messages when an item wasn’t found, and introduced dishes like it was reading data.\n• **The flowchart never ended, and the team couldn’t align.** Voice conversations don’t follow a fixed order, so a screen-style flowchart kept growing and nobody knew where to start.'}]},
{heading:'Discovery',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'With a data analyst, I used AI to analyze thousands of real calls handled by human agents, to see how guests actually order and how staff handle exceptions.'},{imgId:'aca-flow-1',natural:true,title:'Split ordering into one main flow and a list of edge cases, not one giant flowchart',text:'**Decision:** I turned the most common ordering path in the call data into the main flow and listed everything else as edge cases, so the team could decide who handles each one.\n**Trade-off:** There were too many edge cases to discuss in meetings, so we started with the ones that appear most often in calls and left the rarer ones for later.'},{imgId:'aca-flow-2',natural:true,title:'When the AI can’t answer, guide the guest to finish ordering instead of transferring',text:'**Decision:** For off-menu questions, the AI asks follow-ups and guides the guest to finish the order, writes special requests into the order notes, or offers to transfer.\n**Trade-off:** The AI can’t judge whether a note is reasonable for the kitchen, for example whether an ingredient is in stock.'},{noImage:true,title:'Keep payment out of the AI’s hands',text:'**Decision:** Human calls showed that discounts, memberships and payment methods were extremely complex, such as meal credits for delayed flights or discounts bundled with room packages. I put together the reasoning so the PM could convince the hotel not to let the AI handle payment, reducing errors and complaints.\n**Trade-off:** Ordering isn’t fully automated end to end. Staff still confirm payment with the guest, either charging it to the room or bringing a card reader.'}]},
{heading:'Solution',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'**When the AI can’t answer, it guides the guest to finish ordering.**\nSpecial requests go into the notes, or the guest chooses whether to transfer, so the order doesn’t stop.\n\n**When an item isn’t found, it replies in words guests understand.**\nNo more system errors; it explains and suggests alternatives.\n\n**It introduces dishes in a hospitality tone.**\nIt leads with the dish itself, not with phrases that sound like reading data.\n\n**Skipped questions are confirmed once at the end.**\nInstead of chasing each detail right away, a final confirmation fills in what’s missing, so it feels less pushy.\n\n**It gives only what the next step needs.**\nFollowing NN/g’s voice interface guidelines: guests can’t rescan speech, so information comes in small chunks.'},{noImage:true,small:true,title:'Before and after, in five real situations',text:'Before: the AI transferred, read out system errors and asked questions back to back. After: it guides the guest to finish ordering, in a hospitality tone.',chat:{scenarios:[{label:'Menu questions',before:[{who:'guest',text:'Do you have Hainanese chicken rice on the menu?'},{who:'ai',text:'For menu information I need to transfer you to a staff member. Shall I transfer you?'}],after:[{who:'guest',text:'Do you have Hainanese chicken rice on the menu?'},{who:'ai',text:'Yes, we do! Would you like me to order one for you?'}]},{label:'Lookup failures',before:[{who:'guest',text:'I’d like to add a side of fries.'},{who:'ai',text:'Tool call failed.'}],after:[{who:'guest',text:'I’d like to add a side of fries.'},{who:'ai',text:'Sorry, I can’t find fries right now. Would you like to see our other snacks?'}]},{label:'Describing dishes',before:[{who:'guest',text:'Any dessert you’d recommend?'},{who:'ai',text:'I found tiramisu and brownies in the menu.'}],after:[{who:'guest',text:'Any dessert you’d recommend?'},{who:'ai',text:'Our tiramisu is a favorite, and we also have a warm brownie. Which would you like to try?'}]},{label:'No back-to-back questions',before:[{who:'guest',text:'I’d like a steak.'},{who:'ai',text:'How would you like it cooked? Which sauce? What’s your room number?'}],after:[{who:'guest',text:'I’d like a steak.'},{who:'ai',text:'Sure, one steak. How would you like it cooked?'}]},{label:'Special requests',before:[{who:'guest',text:'Can I get the pasta without garlic?'},{who:'ai',text:'I can’t answer that. Let me transfer you to a staff member.'}],after:[{who:'guest',text:'Can I get the pasta without garlic?'},{who:'ai',text:'I’ll note “no garlic” on your order. Or would you like to check with the kitchen directly?'}]}]}}]},
{heading:'Process & the Role of AI',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'Analyze call data → split into a main flow and edge cases → agree on the AI’s scope as a team → confirm service principles with the hotel → write AI rules and test scenarios → validate and refine\n\n• **What AI did:** Analyzed thousands of call logs to surface common ordering paths and issue types; through a Claude Skill I built, it lists the tools and data behind each AI reply in seconds.\n• **What I decided:** Which cases the AI handles and which go to staff; keeping payment away from the AI; when to guide and when to transfer.\n• **What I owned:** The conversation flow, the AI’s service rules written as Do / Don’t, the test scenarios, and finding the root cause of each issue.'},{imgId:'aca-flow-4'},{imgId:'aca-flow-6',imgId2:'aca-flow-6b',natural:true}]},
{heading:'Impact',type:'cards',items:[{compact:true,stat:'0% → 95%',statUnit:'after launch, Crowne Plaza Changi Airport',title:'Orders completed without a transfer, on average',noImage:true},{compact:true,stat:'~600',statUnit:'orders a month, same hotel',title:'Room-service orders completed by the AI each month',noImage:true},{compact:true,title:'The method is being reused',text:'The same approach, from call data to flow design, is now used for restaurant reservations at MHR Hotel Group and Grand Mayfull, currently in development.',noImage:true}]},
{type:'text',items:['**What I would do next:** Keep tracking the complaint rate in the back office, to make sure that as the AI completes more orders, service quality holds.\n\n**The biggest change:** The AI went from a call forwarder to an agent that actually gets work done for the hotel.']}
]},
{id:'guestweb',highlight:'Built a multi-brand design system and a self-serve back office; new-client deployment went from 2 weeks to 1 day',projectType:'UX Research · Design System · Web UI Design',platform:'Web (guest-facing in-room interface + hotel admin back office)',timeline:'2024 – Now',labels:['UX Research','Design System','Web UI Design'],tag:'Multi-brand Website',pairTag:'Content Management System',year:'Systems Thinking',title:'GuestWeb Hotel Digital Concierge',coverHeadline:'Building a hotel guest web page from 0 to 1, cutting new-client deployment from 2 weeks to 1 day',coverSub:'I planned the QR-code web page guests open in their room, and built a multi-brand design system and a back office hotels can use themselves, so every hotel can launch a page in its own brand style without engineering work.',
scope:'• Planned the guest web page from 0 to 1, leading competitive analysis and qualitative interviews\n• Built a tokenized multi-brand design system, defining with frontend engineers what hotels can and can’t change\n• Designed a back office (CMS) hotel staff can build pages with, and the in-room dining menu structure\n• Balanced engineering effort against hotels’ custom requests, proposing customization at the component level',collaborators:'PM · Frontend & backend engineers · Sales · Hotel brand contacts',
oneLiner:'I planned the QR-code web page guests open in their room, and built a multi-brand design system and a back office hotels can use themselves, so every hotel can launch a page in its own brand style without engineering work.',
blocks:[
{heading:'Overview',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'**The situation:** Every hotel wanted a page that fit its brand. Luxury hotels wanted full-bleed images; Japanese hotels wanted dense icons and complete text. Each custom request meant engineering a new template, and deploying a new hotel took about 2 weeks.\n\n**The key decision:** Instead of building a new template for each hotel, put the flexibility into the components, and give hotels a back office they can run themselves.\n\n**The result:** New-client deployment dropped from about 2 weeks to 1 day. At Holiday Inn, the page brings in about 450 F&B orders and NT$270K in revenue a month.'},{noImage:true,text:'This web page is the hotel’s face before, during and after a stay, and the entry point for F&B revenue. If every hotel needs custom development, the engineering load keeps growing and the company can’t sign new hotels quickly.'},{imgId:'guestweb-case-issue',ratio:'1/1',caption:'Before: the MVP had one default style. After: proposals that adapt to each hotel’s brand.'}]},
{heading:'Challenge',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,title:'Hotels weren’t unhappy with the features. One MVP template couldn’t carry their brands.',text:'• **One template couldn’t meet opposite needs.** A luxury hotel complained the MVP didn’t look premium enough and wanted full-bleed images, while Japanese hotels wanted simpler, denser icons.\n• **Every custom request needed engineering.** A new template plus back-and-forth with the hotel meant about 2 weeks to deploy one hotel.\n• **Hotels couldn’t update their own page.** Marketing teams had to go through us to update campaigns, and the people managing content were usually in marketing or IT, without a design background.'}]},
{heading:'Discovery',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'I studied competitors such as Duve, Guestnet, myma.ai and hotelfriend, interviewed hotel staff, and used AI-built mock sites in usability tests. Competitors all offered a high degree of customization, so flexibility was a must; the question was how to offer it without overloading engineering.'},{imgId:'guestweb-case-impl',ratio:'4/3',title:'Customize at the component level, not with a few fixed templates',text:'**Decision:** My first proposal was a few fixed templates, but engineering worried about the development load, and they weren’t flexible enough. I switched to components that switch between text-and-image styles, so engineering builds once and covers both full-bleed images and dense icons.\n**Trade-off:** Hotels choose from designed styles rather than designing their own layout freely.'},{imgId:'guestweb-3-1',ratio:'1.9/1',title:'One brand color for hotels; system colors stay locked',text:'**Decision:** I built a tokenized design system and agreed with frontend engineers on which components hotels can change and which system colors stay fixed. One brand color expands into a full color scale.\n**Trade-off:** Hotels can’t recolor every component as they like, in exchange for a consistent, readable interface.'},{noImage:true,title:'Give up free layout for a grid',text:'**Decision:** I first wanted Webflow-style free layout, but engineers pointed out that calculating widths on the fly would be costly to maintain. I switched to a grid with a few fixed sizes, and the back office shows how much space is left.\n**Trade-off:** Hotels lose a little layout freedom, and engineering maintenance stays under control.',media:'slot-assets/guestweb-builder.mp4',poster:'slot-assets/guestweb-builder-poster.webp',mediaRatio:'1400/840'}]},
{heading:'Solution',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'**New hotels go live in 1 day instead of about 2 weeks.**\nPages are built in the back office, by the hotel itself or with our team’s help, with no new template to develop.\n\n**One brand color restyles the whole interface.**\nThe token system expands it into a full color scale, while system colors stay readable.\n\n**The same components serve luxury and business hotels.**\nEach component switches between text-and-image styles, so one codebase covers opposite needs.\n\n**Page editing and menu management follow different flows.**\nPage editing goes from large to small; menu management goes from small to large, matching each task’s mental model.\n\n**A four-level menu structure, defined with the PM.**\nMenu, category, item and customization group keep ordering simple for guests and manageable for staff.'},{imgId:'guestweb-mental-model',ratio:'4/3',caption:'Two opposite mental models: page editing (large to small) and menu management (small to large).'},{imgId:'guestweb-dining-flow',natural:true},{imgId:'guestweb-3-1-r',natural:true}]},
{heading:'Process & the Role of AI',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'Competitive research and interviews → define what can be customized → build the tokenized design system → test the back office with AI-built mock sites → align grid and component specs with engineering → launch and watch the data\n\n• **What AI did:** Quickly built working mock sites for interviews and internal usability tests.\n• **What I decided:** Customizing at the component level, locking system colors, a grid instead of free layout, and separate flows for page editing and menus.\n• **What I owned:** Competitive analysis and interviews, design system specs, guest web and back-office design, and the menu structure with the PM.'},{pairIds:['guestweb-3-0b','guestweb-3-0c']},{imgId:'guestweb-3-0d',natural:true,caption:'Using an AI-built mock website to test users’ mental models when operating the back-office system'}]},
{heading:'Impact',type:'cards',items:[{compact:true,stat:'2 weeks → 1 day',statUnit:'from about 2 weeks',title:'New-client deployment time',noImage:true},{compact:true,stat:'NT$270K',statUnit:'monthly F&B revenue',title:'Holiday Inn, about 450 F&B orders a month',noImage:true},{compact:true,stat:'NT$160K',statUnit:'monthly F&B revenue',title:'iHotel, 490 orders a month, NT$326 average order',noImage:true},{compact:true,stat:'384',statUnit:'calls saved',title:'Hotel Clover, phone calls saved in one month',noImage:true}]},
{type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,small:true,title:'Hotel Feedback',text:'“This channel lets us engage with guests in a better way — not only keeping them up to date on the latest offers in real time, but also ensuring they never miss any information, for a smoother, more engaging stay.” — Holiday Inn & Suites Saigon Airport.\n“Since we started working together, our F&B revenue has grown 30× compared to the same period, and the Aiello Voice Assistant is the best low-friction ordering tool on the market today.” — iHotel.'}]},
{type:'text',items:['**Reflection:** Custom home pages and menus also have to match each hotel’s ordering system data. Design isn’t only about how screens look; the data underneath has to line up.\n\n**The biggest change:** Every hotel can launch a page in its own brand style in a day, without waiting on engineering.']},
{type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'guestweb-3-1b'}]},
]},
{id:'tms-ds',coverNatural:true,timeline:'2024 – Now',highlight:'Built an interactive AI prototype in one week on top of a new design system, helping sales win a Japanese listed group',projectType:'Design System · AI Workflow · Sales Enablement',platform:'Web back office',timelineFirst:true,labels:['Design System','AI Workflow','Sales Enablement'],title:'TMS Back-Office Design System',coverHeadline:'An interactive AI-built prototype in one week, helping sales win a Japanese listed group',coverSub:'I first turned a back office without standards into a design system and component library, then used it to build an interactive proposal prototype in one week, with a solution that met the client’s need without losing the product’s focus.',
scope:'• Reframed the client’s request with the PM and sales, proposing a solution that kept the product’s focus\n• Planned the prototype flow and limited the AI to building with the existing component library\n• Audited components from the live product and built a design system defined by usage scenarios\n• Turned the specs into design tokens and an HTML component library with Figma MCP and Claude Code',collaborators:'PM · Frontend engineers · Sales',
oneLiner:'I first turned a back office without standards into a design system and component library, then used it to build an interactive proposal prototype in one week, with a solution that met the client’s need without losing the product’s focus.',
blocks:[
{heading:'Overview',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'**The situation:** The hotel business of a Japanese listed financial group (10+ hotels, about 3,500 rooms) reported internally in Excel, and wanted to bring Excel’s views and workflow into our task management product. We had one week for the proposal.\n\n**The key decision:** Instead of turning the product into Excel, keep its task management focus: a default view, plus a switch to see full task details. Then use AI with our existing component library to build a working prototype in a week.\n\n**The result:** Sales shared the screen in the meeting and sent the link so the client could try it on the spot. The client agreed during the meeting, then signed and went live.'},{noImage:true,text:'We’re a SaaS product: one product serves many hotels. Building Excel-style views for one client could hurt the experience for existing hotels, and once we head toward Excel, we’d need many more features to avoid losing to Excel itself. And with one week, static mockups couldn’t show the client how the flow would actually feel.'}]},
{heading:'Challenge',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,title:'Building exactly what the client asked for would have cost the SaaS product its focus',text:'• **The client was used to Excel.** They reported in Excel internally and wanted its views and workflow inside our product.\n• **A SaaS product can’t change for one client.** Other hotels might not need this experience, and it would pull the product away from task management.\n• **One week, and inconsistent components.** Built fast to validate the product, the back office had 3–4 styles per component, and the design files didn’t match what was live, so there was no solid base to assemble a prototype quickly.'}]},
{heading:'Discovery',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'I discussed the client’s real need with the PM and sales, and audited the live back office, using an AI-written crawler and Playwright to record the component styles that had actually shipped.'},{noImage:true,title:'Keep the task management focus, and make full details a switchable view',text:'**Decision:** Keep the default view, and add an option to switch to a view with full task details, so the client gets what they need without turning the product into Excel.\n**Trade-off:** It adds a step to switch views. In return, the product keeps its character, and we planned to remember each user’s view choice so they rarely need to switch.',media:'slot-assets/tms-ds-proposal-prototype.mp4',poster:'slot-assets/tms-ds-proposal-prototype-poster.webp',mediaRatio:'1400/672'},{noImage:true,title:'Let the AI build only with the existing component library',text:'**Decision:** I planned the flow first and told the AI to build the prototype pages using only the component library we had already built, so it couldn’t invent components or interactions that would cause trouble once engineering started.\n**Trade-off:** The proposal could only show what existing components allow, with no freedom to design new ones just for the pitch. In return, what the client saw was what engineering could actually build.'},{imgId:'tms-ds-flow',natural:true},{noImage:true,title:'Define each component by scenario first, then style, based on the live product',text:'**Decision:** Instead of starting from old mockups, I audited what was live and defined each component by the situation it’s used in, how users behave there, and which style follows. The 3–4 variants per component became one.\n**Trade-off:** The reasoning behind some old components could no longer be traced, so a few may now behave differently from how they were once used. Every new component has a clear scenario behind it, and I keep tracking usage feedback.'},{noImage:true,gallery:[{label:'Dialog',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-dialog.png'},{label:'Alert Dialog',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-alert.png'},{label:'Date Picker',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-picker.png'},{label:'List Item',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-list.png'},{label:'Table',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-table.png'},{label:'Table Types',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-table-types-v2.png'}],caption:'Each component spec lists its anatomy, its variants, and the scenarios each variant is used in.'}]},
{heading:'Solution',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'**A working prototype in one week.**\nSales shared it in the meeting and sent the link, so the client could filter, open and edit tasks on the spot.\n\n**The default view stays; full details are one switch away.**\nThe product keeps its task management focus while meeting the client’s need for complete information.\n\n**Each component has one spec.**\nDesigners no longer dig through old files, and engineers no longer compare different versions of the same component.\n\n**The design system became a library engineers can use.**\nTokens and a clickable HTML component library, plus Markdown brand guidelines engineers follow when generating pages.',media:'slot-assets/tms-ds-component-library.mp4',poster:'slot-assets/tms-ds-component-library-poster.webp',mediaRatio:'1280/688'}]},
{heading:'Process & the Role of AI',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,text:'Reframe the need with the PM and sales → plan the flow → the AI assembles the prototype from the component library only → sales demos it in the meeting → the client agrees and signs\n\nUnderneath: audit the live product with an AI-written crawler → bring live components back into Figma with a plugin → define components by scenario → convert specs into tokens and a component library with Figma MCP and Claude Code\n\n• **What AI did:** Wrote the crawler that recorded live component styles; converted specs into design tokens and an HTML component library; assembled the prototype pages from that library, following the flow I planned.\n• **What I decided:** Not turning the product into Excel and offering a switchable view instead; using the live product as the source of truth; each component’s scenarios and style; limiting the AI to existing components.\n• **What I owned:** The prototype flow, the component audit and specs, the Figma plugin, and the Markdown brand guidelines for engineers.'}]},
{heading:'Impact',type:'cards',items:[{compact:true,stat:'1',statUnit:'week',title:'From the client’s request to a working prototype',noImage:true},{compact:true,stat:'10+',statUnit:'hotels, about 3,500 rooms',title:'The Japanese listed group’s hotel business, won',noImage:true},{compact:true,stat:'3–4 → 1',statUnit:'styles per component',title:'Styles per component, unified into one spec',noImage:true}]},
{type:'text',items:['**Reflection:** A design system has to stay in sync with what is live. When the product departs from the spec, I record the exception as a reference for the next change, rather than letting the two drift apart.\n\n**The biggest change:** The design system stopped being just a spec. It became the tool that helped sales win a key account in a week.']}
]},
{id:'nexus',highlight:'Shipped in 2021',dark:true,coverNatural:true,coverHeadline:'Readable at a glance, built to look the part.',projectType:'UI Design · Hardware–Software Integration',timeline:'2020',platform:'Streaming controller panel + desktop software',timelineFirst:true,labels:['UI Design','Hardware–Software Integration','Motion Design'],title:'Live Streamer NEXUS',
oneLiner:'A streaming controller and its companion software. Starting from how streamers actually use it mid-broadcast, I built a visual system that makes every state readable at a glance on a small screen, without giving up the bold esports look the product needed.',
scope:'Dynamic UI components for the hardware panel and desktop software, boot animation, LED lighting scripts, icon library, UI string translation',
blocks:[
{heading:'Background',type:'text',title:'Streamers glance at the controller. They don’t study it.',items:['NEXUS is AVerMedia’s streaming software, paired with the Live Streamer NEXUS controller. Streamers use its touchscreen and knobs to switch capture sources, mix audio and trigger hotkeys.','Mid-stream, attention is on the game and the audience; the controller gets only a glance. So every button has to show what it does and whether it’s on, in that split second.']},
{type:'cards',items:[{title:'Before',text:'Mid-stream, streamers often had to open several programs and adjust audio levels and aspect ratios'},{title:'With NEXUS',text:'Preset multiple streaming scenes, then swipe the controller’s screen to switch between them instantly'}]},
{heading:'Project at a Glance',pin:1,type:'cards',items:[
{compact:true,noImage:true,stat:'2021',statUnit:'mass-produced',title:'Shipped in 2021',text:'Launched as a mass-produced product: AVerMedia Live Streamer NEXUS (model AX310).'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'State readable at a glance',text:'Hotkeys pair each app’s brand color with motion: on is full color and animated, off dims to a dark tone.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'One look across hardware and software',text:'The same components run on the controller’s touchscreen and in the desktop app, backed by an icon library with five interaction states.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'A complete visual asset system',text:'A boot animation with a millisecond-timed LED script, custom hotkey icons, and an app icon in 9 sizes from 16 to 1024 px.'}
]},
{heading:'Problems & Challenges',type:'bullets',items:['A glance, not a gaze: streamers look down for a split second mid-stream, so state has to register instantly','A small screen: on the controller’s touchscreen, subtle changes in an icon’s shape are easy to miss','Same function, different channels: once NEXUS integrated platforms like OBS and Twitch, buttons with the same function but for different channels could appear on the same page, and had to be told apart at a glance','Two displays, one component: the same UI appears on the hardware touchscreen and in the desktop software','Clarity without losing flair: this is a product for streamers and gamers, so it also has to look bold and polished']},
{heading:'Design Decisions',type:'text-image',items:[
{title:'Designing for a glance: from text labels to color and motion',text:'The original icons marked state with tiny ON / OFF text, easy to miss on a small screen. After adding OBS and Twitch, same-function buttons for different channels also had to be told apart.\n\nMy first version used color families for channels and built the state into the icon. My manager and fellow designers felt the contrast should be stronger for quick mid-stream use.\n\nIn the final version, each hotkey uses its app’s brand color: full color and animated when on, dimmed when off. The state reads even from the corner of the eye.',noImage:true,iconCols:5,iconGrid:['slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-on-1-mixer.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-on-2-share.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-on-3-layers.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-on-4-camera.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-on-5-record.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-off-1-mixer.png','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-off-2-layout.png','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-off-3-layers.png','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-off-4-store.png','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-off-5-record.png','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-blue-1-folder.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-blue-2-mixer.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-blue-3-sound.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-blue-4-seat.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-blue-5-play.gif']},
{title:'• Customizable, without breaking the system',text:'In NEXUS’s online editor, streamers can set their own hotkey backgrounds and images. I drew icon sets in several styles so personalized layouts still look polished.',noImage:true,iconCols:5,iconGrid:['slot-assets/nexus-custom-1-guitar.png','slot-assets/nexus-custom-2-award.png','slot-assets/nexus-custom-3-kaomoji.png','slot-assets/nexus-custom-4-moon.png','slot-assets/nexus-custom-5-device.png']},
{title:'One visual language across hardware and software',text:'The desktop app uses a dark base to match the controller. I drew a clean line-icon library and specified five interaction states: Normal, Mouse over, Click, Disable and Focus.',imgId:'nexus-icon-library',natural:true},
{title:'Visual flair that carries the brand: boot animation and lighting',text:'Clarity sets the rules, but the product should also excite at power-on. With a flashy esports concept, I animated the AVerMedia logo for the boot screen and designed LED effects that flash in sync.',imgId:'nexus-boot',natural:true},
{title:'• Lighting scripts timed to the millisecond',text:'The lighting changes fast, so I scripted each pattern precisely, millisecond by millisecond.',imgId:'nexus-light-script',natural:true},
{title:'• App icon',text:'Based on the controller’s knobs and the software’s signature orange, I drew the app icon in 9 sizes, from 16 to 1024 px.',imgId:'nexus-app-icon',natural:true}
]},
{heading:'Final Product',pin:2,type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'nexus-final',natural:true}]},
{heading:'Reflection',type:'text',items:['NEXUS taught me to design hardware and software as one experience. The lights, the touchscreen and the desktop settings all answer one question: what does the streamer need to know right now?','If I did it again, I would test hotkeys in real streams, not only design reviews, to confirm the dimmed off state stays readable under real lighting.']}
]},
{id:'1177pay',highlight:'Improved bill payment, donations and card binding in a consumer payment app, plus widgets and loading motion',coverNatural:true,projectType:'UI Design · B2C Mobile Payment',timeline:'2021 – 2023',platform:'Mobile App',timelineFirst:true,coverHeadline:'Making bill payment quick and easy, starting from the very first payment.',labels:['UI Design','B2C Mobile Payment'],title:'1177PAY Mobile Payment App',
scope:'Bill details, donation page, card-binding flow, widget, loading animation, flowcharts, Design Guideline maintenance',collaborators:'PO · Other designers',
blocks:[
{heading:'Background',type:'text',items:['1177PAY is a mobile payment app. Users link credit cards, bank accounts and e-invoice barcodes to pay quickly, and get bills and payment confirmations through push notifications.','Goal: make paying bills fast, and turn first-time users of the company’s payment services into loyal app members.','Several designers worked on the app. I owned bill details, the donation page, the card-binding flow, and details like the widget and loading animation.']},
{heading:'Project at a Glance',pin:1,type:'cards',items:[
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'Bills and donations',text:'Reordered the information in bill details, and redesigned the main screen for browsing charities on the donation page.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'Easier card binding',text:'Showed each card’s issuing bank logo, and improved the camera screen for scanning a credit card and filling in its details automatically.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'Visual details',text:'A widget in light and dark mode, a loading animation made in After Effects, and the App Store screenshots.'}
]},
{heading:'Problems & Challenges',type:'bullets',items:['Converting first-time users: many people meet 1177PAY through the company’s payment services rather than the app store, so the app has to show its value right away.','Complex payment flows: payments involve many checkpoints and branches, which have to be documented clearly enough for engineers to build.','Trust when adding a card: linking a credit card is a sensitive step, so users need to recognize their card and understand what the camera is doing.','Keeping a growing product consistent: as new features are added, the Design Guideline has to stay up to date so every screen still feels like the same product.']},
{heading:'Design Decisions',type:'text-image',items:[
{title:'Turning requirements into screens',text:'Working with the PO, I designed screens from the wireframes and wrote the design specifications.',imgId:'pay-screens',natural:true},
{title:'• Making card binding easier to trust',text:'Each card shows its bank’s logo so users recognize it instantly. I also made the card-scanning camera screen easier to understand and use.',noImage:true},
{title:'• Bill details and donations',text:'I reordered the information in bill details, and redesigned the main screen where users browse charities on the donation page.',noImage:true},
{title:'• Widget design',text:'Designed in both light and dark mode.',imgId:'pay-widget',natural:true},
{title:'• Loading motion design',text:'Inspired by a wallet’s snap button opening, I created the loading animation in After Effects.',imgId:'pay-loading',natural:true},
{title:'• Color palette',text:'Built on the blue of the company’s corporate identity, paired with grays and whites for a clean, trustworthy feel.',imgId:'pay-color',natural:true},
{title:'Flowcharts',text:'From the finished screens, I drew flowcharts marking checkpoints and screen transitions.',imgId:'pay-flow',natural:true},
{title:'Design handoff',text:'I delivered the designs and sliced assets through Sketch and Zeplin.',imgId:'pay-handoff',natural:true}
]},
{heading:'Results',type:'text',items:['I delivered bill details, the donation page, the card-binding flow, the widget, the loading animation, flowcharts and App Store screenshots, and kept the Design Guideline current.']},
{heading:'Reflection',type:'text',items:['In a payment product, trust is built in the details: a familiar bank logo on each card, a clear state at every checkpoint, and reminders that arrive before users have to think about their bills.']},
{heading:'Final Product',pin:2,type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'pay-feature-pay',natural:true},{imgId:'pay-feature-push',imgId2:'pay-feature-scan',natural:true}]}
]},
{id:'17backstage',highlight:'Redesigned the merchant back office with a new design guideline; leadership adopted it across every product line',coverNatural:true,projectType:'UI Design · B2B Back Office',timeline:'2021 – 2023',platform:'Web back office',timelineFirst:true,coverHeadline:'Turning a templated back office into a clear, consistent system for small merchants.',labels:['UI Design','B2B Back Office','Redesign'],title:'1177 Tech Merchant Back Office',
scope:'Redesign proposal, layout and interaction design, Design Guideline, wireframes, mockups and handoff',collaborators:'Company leadership · Product manager · Frontend engineers',
blocks:[
{heading:'Background',type:'text',title:'Small merchants needed a simpler way to accept payments',items:['1177 Tech provides third-party payment collection, letting merchants accept credit cards, convenience-store payments and virtual-account transfers on their own online store platforms.','The back office was an MVP for small merchants and charities that need to collect payments but don’t want to apply for a card terminal or LINE Pay. The goal: quick to sign up, intuitive to use.','The first version was built on a template, and leadership found it hard to use. As the company’s only UI designer, I led the redesign.']},
{heading:'Project at a Glance',pin:1,type:'cards',items:[
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'Proposal adopted company-wide',text:'Leadership chose my redesign proposal and asked me to apply its design guideline to every product line in the company.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,stat:'1',statUnit:'designer, end to end',title:'The only designer, end to end',text:'From the proposal and the Design Guideline to wireframes, mockups and handoff, I designed the whole system.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,stat:'3',statUnit:'layout improvements',title:'Three layout improvements',text:'A top menu set apart from content, detail pages shown as popups, and a collapsible step bar for long forms.'}
]},
{heading:'Problems & Challenges',type:'bullets',items:['Too much information: lots of data, and users need to find features fast.','Unclear hierarchy: a vertical menu next to vertical data lists looked like the same level.','Endless scrolling: details were listed one per line, making pages very long while the right half of the screen sat mostly empty.','Cramped forms: the step bar on the left of form pages squeezed the width of the form itself.','Easy-to-miss errors: errors in the application form were shown only as text, so users could easily overlook them.']},
{heading:'Design Decisions',type:'text-image',items:[
{title:'Leading with the reasoning: a proposal built around how people read',text:'Instead of just new screens, I presented a deck explaining why each component sits where it does: following reading order, guiding the eye, and making risky actions clear. Leadership chose it, and it became the basis of the new Design Guideline.',noImage:true},
{title:'Reorganizing the screen layout',text:'The old left-side menu looked too similar to the vertical data lists. I moved it to the top, with a shadow and a different background to separate it from the content.\n\nI considered keeping the side menu, but with so many items it would take too much space and pull focus from the content.',imgId:'bs-layout',natural:true},
{title:'Rearranging detail pages, so users no longer scroll endlessly',text:'Detail pages listed one item per line, making pages long with an empty right half. We split information into columns with wrapping rules by field length, and turned details into a popup so users can compare records easily.',imgId:'bs-detail',natural:true},
{title:'Consolidating form pages',text:'The step bar on the left squeezed the form. I made it expandable and collapsible, so users jump straight to the section they need.',imgId:'bs-form',natural:true},
{title:'• Earlier, clearer error messages',text:'Errors used to be text only. Now users are warned before moving on, with an icon, and each field shows its progress and state.',imgId:'bs-form-hints',natural:true},
{title:'A new Design Guideline',text:'From the proposal, I built a shared component library to keep systems consistent. Leadership applied it to every product line, and the visual designer used it for marketing too.',pairIds:['bs-guide-input','bs-guide-dropdown','bs-guide-icon-btn','bs-guide-font']},
{title:'Discussing wireframes',text:'I used wireframes to discuss component usage and screen layouts with the product manager.',imgId:'bs-wireframe',natural:true},
{title:'Building mockups',text:'I designed the screens in Sketch and uploaded them to Zeplin for handoff to the frontend engineers.',noImage:true}
]},
{heading:'Final Product',pin:2,type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'bs-final-1',natural:true},{imgId:'bs-final-2',natural:true},{imgId:'bs-final-3',natural:true},{imgId:'bs-final-4',natural:true},{imgId:'bs-final-5',natural:true}]},
{heading:'Results',type:'text',items:['The back office was still an MVP, so the impact was internal: leadership adopted my proposal, the Design Guideline went company-wide, and the visual designer used it for marketing.']},
{heading:'Reflection',type:'text',items:['A redesign wins on its reasoning, not just its screens. Explaining layout through reading order, visual flow and risk is what convinced leadership, and turned one redesign into a company-wide guideline.']}
]}
];
